NNAAMMEE
     rraadd — router advertisement daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     rraadd [--ddnnvv] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--ss _s_o_c_k_e_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     rraadd is an IPv6 router advertisement daemon.  It periodically sends IPv6
     router advertisement messages with prefix and default router information.
     Clients like slaacd(8) use these to configure IPv6 addresses on network
     interfaces and set default routes.  Additionally it listens for IPv6
     router solicitation messages and responds with router advertisements.

     To have rraadd enabled at boot time, use “rcctl enable rad”, which sets

           rad_flags=""

     in rc.conf.local(8).

     A running rraadd can be controlled with the ractl(8) utility.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, rraadd will run in
             the foreground and log to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Specify an alternative configuration file.

     --nn      Configtest mode.  Only check the configuration file for validity.

     --ss _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Use an alternate location for the default control socket.

     --vv      Produce more verbose output.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/rad.conf        Default rraadd configuration file.
     /var/run/rad.sock    UNIX-domain socket used for communication with
                          ractl(8).

